course: dictionary
lesson: 5413
title: A word with a twisty story
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A labyrinth is a maze, a network of paths or passages where it is easy to get lost. Some words have more than one meaning, so the clues around them tell you which meaning is used. «Theseus went into the labyrinth to fight the Minotaur.» The words fight, Minotaur and story show you it is the old Greek maze.
explain: The word can also mean something very complicated and confusing, like «a labyrinth of rules». Nobody is lost in a real maze there, but the rules are so twisted that they are hard to follow. Look at the words around it: lost, rules, confusing.
explain: Sometimes the word is a science word. The labyrinth in your ear is the inner part that helps you hear and keep your balance. A church or garden may also have a single winding path for walking and thinking. Each time, the context tells you which meaning to use.
fact: A labyrinth is a complicated network of paths or passages where it is easy to get lost.
fact: A labyrinth is another word for a maze.
fact: In a Greek myth, the maze on the island of Crete where the Minotaur lived was called the labyrinth.
fact: «Clue» comes from «clew», a ball of thread; Theseus used a thread to find his way out of the labyrinth.
fact: The labyrinth in the inner ear helps with hearing and balance.
exercise: «Theseus went into the labyrinth to fight the Minotaur.» Which meaning of labyrinth fits here? | A loud noise in the street | Lost in a garden shed | A small pond with ducks | *A maze on the island of Crete
exercise: The new school rules are a labyrinth, so nobody understands them. What does labyrinth mean here? | A fast, cold river | A big yellow school bus | *Something very complicated and confusing | A soft woolly blanket
exercise: In your ear, the labyrinth helps you with what? | Seeing colours | Cooking and baking | *Hearing and balance | Running fast
test: Why did Theseus use a ball of thread in the labyrinth? | *To find his way back out again | To play a game with the Minotaur | To tie up his shoes | To draw a map of the walls
test: Which word is a synonym for labyrinth in its first meaning? | Crete | Balance | Thread | *Maze
exam: In «She was lost in a labyrinth of streets», which word is the best clue that labyrinth means a maze? | *lost | streets | she | of
